#### case 80 lentBorrows
#### Where the function performs an operation that a handler around the call
#### handles, the borrow cannot be a scope that lasts as long as the call:
#### the handler would run while it is set aside. It is made before the
#### handler is there, and lasts until the handler is gone.
effect Tick { tick() }
    fn work(p: &mut Int) -> Int / {Tick} { tick(); *p = *p + 1; tick(); *p }
    fn main() -> Int { let mut n = 0; handle work(&mut n) { tick(k) => k(()) } }
#### expect
some 1
#### case 93 lentBorrows
#### A handler that itself reaches another part of the variable: the variable
#### is one per field, since its fields are borrowed apart, and the borrow of
#### one is a scope that the other's use does not touch.
effect Tick { tick() }
    fn work(p: &mut Int) -> Int / {Tick} { tick(); *p = *p + 1; tick(); *p }
    fn main() -> Int { let mut n = (0, 5); handle work(&mut n.0) { tick(k) => print(n.1); k(()) } }
#### expect
some 1
#### case 81 lentBorrows
#### A callable made before the call can be given the borrow: its type binds
#### the region of the reference it takes.
